        "id": 1081395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-11T09:49:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "this seems like something we should get/set in WifiNative and then ask if it is supported (and then hold that value or keep asking.  It knows best when the features should be updated/checked",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3945,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 3945,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "c7683ab80db0970e6422d9405c99b5f67d951fc8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e30fc9c9_08f65462",
        "filename": "service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiStateMachine.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 3945,
      "author": {
        "id": 1086611
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-12T06:52:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "WifiNative is a thin pass-thru layer which glues WifiHAL with the framework. I don\u0027t think we want to push too much logic there. It\u0027s a stateless layer. It doesn\u0027t and shouldn\u0027t know about the state. Thus framework layer has better knowledge of when the features should be checked. BTW, being stateless is why the current hybrid WifiNative (mix of HIDL and legacy HAL) works.",
      "parentUuid": "646545c3_2f21dd80",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3945,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 3945,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "c7683ab80db0970e6422d9405c99b5f67d951fc8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f9469158_71c085d3",
        "filename": "service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiStateMachine.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 3945,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-14T18:50:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was thinking something more like this:\n public RttManager.RttCapabilities getRttCapabilities() {\n\nso you could do something similar with firmwareRoaming - like the method you already have, but then it would be in WifiNative.  This would allow us 2 benefits:\n1 - wifiNative can control the implementation - the first pass could be getting the supported features on demand, like you have it here.  it can be optimized later in WifiNative, but WifiStateMachine won\u0027t care.\n2 - if this is an expensive operation, then it would be better for wifiNative to control it and allow more than just getting info for firmwareRoaming info, it can get other info.",
      "parentUuid": "e30fc9c9_08f65462",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3945,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 3945,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "c7683ab80db0970e6422d9405c99b5f67d951fc8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "81bd1b13_388ed578",
        "filename": "service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiStateMachine.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 3945,
      "author": {
        "id": 1086611
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-14T22:28:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "getRttCapabilities() is different. It has a 1:1 mapping with getRttCapabilitiesNative() and its correspondent HAL API. We have its equivalent tWifiNative.getRoamingCapabilities() for roaming.\n\nAll we are doing here is to take the existing WifiNative.getSupportedFeatureSet() API and get what features the firmware supports. And it\u0027s done only once after firmware gets (re)loaded. From there we figure out whether the firmware supports controlled roaming. Current implementation has a couple of benefits:\n\n1) Again, keep WifiNative as simple as possible. And it is consistent with what we do today with other HAL APIs.\n\n2) Instead of just getting if the firmware supports this particular feature, we now have the complete picture of what features this firmware supports. Later if needed, we can check if the firmware supports MAC randomization without extra cost, for example. Again, I don\u0027t think it makes sense for WifiNative to figure out when it should query HAL to get the feature set value and maintain a copy of it. That breaks the current model.",
      "parentUuid": "f9469158_71c085d3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3945,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 3945,
        "endChar": 80
      },
